              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 3.16: Types of joints"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.384,
            0.089,
            0.622,
            0.113
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3.5 Types of Joints"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.384,
            0.12,
            0.673,
            0.14
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3.5.1 Ball and socket joint"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.382,
            0.142,
            0.89,
            0.279
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The shoulder joint allows free movement of the arm (Table 3.5). This is because the rounded top of the upper arm bone fits into a shallow hollow of the shoulder bone, forming the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "ball and socket joint",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (Fig. 3.16). Together with the collarbone, the shoulder forms the shoulder girdle, which connects the arm to the skeleton. This joint allows forward, backwards, sideways and circular movements."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.384,
            0.287,
            0.572,
            0.308
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3.5.2 Hinge joint"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.382,
            0.31,
            0.89,
            0.396
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Unlike the shoulder, the elbow bends and straightens in one direction only like a door hinge (Table 3.5). This type of joint is called a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "hinge joint",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". A similar hinge joint is present in the knee, where a small bone called the kneecap protects the joint."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.024,
            0.417,
            0.088,
            0.484
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 7,
              "bbox": [
                0.024,
                0.417,
                0.088,
                0.484
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_12_image_7.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.09,
            0.445,
            0.3,
            0.465
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ready to Go Beyond"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.068,
            0.475,
            0.356,
            0.6
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Stem cells in the bone marrow are special cells that can divide and make new cells. In a bone marrow transplant, stem cells from a healthy person are given to patients who have blood cancers like Leukemia or disorders, such as Thalassemia."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.384,
            0.404,
            0.564,
            0.424
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3.5.3 Pivot joint"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.382,
            0.426,
            0.89,
            0.529
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now, try shaking your head ‘no.’ Place your hand at the back of your neck and feel the gentle movement. How does the neck move so freely? The skull is connected to the backbone through a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "pivot joint",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", which allows the head to move side to side like a doorknob turning in its socket."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.383,
            0.537,
            0.579,
            0.558
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3.5.4 Fixed joints"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.382,
            0.56,
            0.89,
            0.646
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The skull is a hard case of flat bones joined together to protect the brain, eyes and ears. The bones of the skull are connected by "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "fixed joints",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", which means the bones of the skull cannot move. This keeps the brain safe even when the body moves."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.035,
            0.643,
            0.096,
            0.676
          ],
          "content": [
